t(25, 1830, """613
The decisive battle in the Persians' victory over the Byzantines takes place
614
The seizure of the nearest land and of Jerusalem

The Byzantines begin to recover
622
The first tactical victory over the Persians in Anatolia
627
The Byzantine army crushes the Persian army at the Battle of Nineveh

In 624 the Muslims rejoiced at their victory at the Battle of Badr, while in the same year Heraclius swept over wide lands that the Persians had taken and recovered them as part of his march of victories.

The military and political events in the Arabian Peninsula during the first years of the Hijrah ran parallel to the grinding conflict between the Byzantines and the Persians, and formed a firm strategic link that changed the map of the old world, paving the way for the Muslims to appear in history for the first time, and for the ground to be prepared for them so that their victories became easier for them.""")

t(26, 55, """The following is information explaining the events of today's world, which we explain to a group of our deluded brothers, some of whose comments we see here from time to time, and who link the victory of the Ummah with the inevitability of the verse of the Smoke occurring, deluding themselves that victory is impossible to happen except with the occurrence of the verse of the Smoke!
------------------------
1. The linked events in the Arabian Peninsula (the first years of the Hijrah)
* Historical and psychological synchronicity (year 2 AH / 624 AD):
At the same time that Heraclius was advancing deep into Persian lands and destroying the fire temple in Azerbaijan (the first great strategic victory for the Byzantines), the Muslims were achieving their first resounding military victory at the Battle of Badr against Quraysh. This synchronicity fulfilled the Qur'anic prophecy of the Byzantines' victory, raised the morale of the Muslims, and consolidated the standing of the nascent Islamic state in the heart of the Arabian Peninsula as a rising power not to be underestimated.
* The Treaty of Hudaybiyyah as a political turning point (year 6 AH / 628 AD):
This treaty coincided with the decisive Battle of Nineveh (627-628 AD), in which the Byzantines completely crushed the Persians. Politically, this treaty gave the Muslims a military truce with Quraysh, and the Prophet ﷺ used this political stability to address the kings of the world (among them Heraclius the Byzantine emperor, Khosrow the king of Persia, and al-Muqawqis the ruler of Egypt), proclaiming the universality of the Islamic call and the entry of Islam as a political player on the international stage for the first time.

2. How did these events later make the Muslims' victory over the Persians and Byzantines easier?

These shared conditions paved the way for the great Islamic conquests in the era of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq and Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with them) through three basic axes:

| The strategic axis |
The situation of the Persians and Byzantines | The effect and the facilitation in favour of the Muslims |

| Military and economic exhaustion |
The war between them continued for nearly 26 years without break (602 - 628 AD), and in it the two empires drained all their financial and human resources, and their cities and fortresses were destroyed.

The Muslims faced armies exhausted and drained psychologically and materially, which made their lines of defence fragile and easy to penetrate before a young Islamic army with a high fighting creed and renewed energy.

| Vacuum and political fragmentation |
After the Persians' defeat in 628 AD, Khosrow was assassinated and the Sasanian state entered sweeping political chaos (about 10 rulers took power within a few years). As for the Byzantines, they faced religious division and persecution of the Christians of the Levant and Egypt.

The Muslims exploited the political disintegration in Persia to conquer Iraq and bring down the empire. In the Levant, the local population welcomed the Muslims and opened the cities to them, fleeing the injustice of the Byzantines.""")

