t(29, 1550, """In consultation cases that have reached me many times, when a person is too cowardly to confront, he harasses!

The reason for being too cowardly to confront is not that the person is a coward. The matter has nothing to do with cowardice in the sense of fear! The matter has another context.

There are many "idol-like" ideas and beliefs in minds that are the product of a mental, psychological, intellectual and behavioural "collective" structure built by the collective environment. It is the product of an error not guided by the Book and the Sunnah and contrary to the Book and the Sunnah. On its basis the idolised person builds in his chest a burden and a load that weighs on him and for which there is no need. Because of this delusion in his head, born of mistaken collective social beliefs in his society, he cannot take the burdens from his chest and throw them far away to find relief, and he finds no way to get around his inability to confront his social idol except to embark on conduct containing harm and abuse, so that perhaps what has settled on his chest will dissolve a little and stir, and he will find relief!

This mistaken act is committed by a huge number of people. They build idols within themselves and delude themselves with delusions that have no existence except in their minds, and these delusions leave them in constant fatigue and toil, and they push themselves towards committing mistakes, many of which are mistakes that ought not to happen and do not befit those who commit them, since they are diminished by them far, far more than one thinks.

So confronting the idols, illusions and delusions that society builds and plants in the mind of each individual about the meaning of manhood and the meaning of values of various kinds, where the acquired social meanings are customary and have nothing to do with the Book and the Sunnah, confronting these idols is a thousand times better than committing follies!

The reason for not confronting these value idols connected to wrong meanings of values in minds is the failure of the individual misled by his society in the ability to re-educate himself, and his failure in the ability to be independent with his mind and remove the influence of the herd from himself.

I conclude with the example that reached me:
A man whose idolised herd society taught him that the inheritance of his sisters was his right and the right of his male brothers""")

t(30, 60, """with him, but he feared being labelled with a bad label, so that it would be said of him and his brothers that they are not men. So he could not consume the girls' wealth, and kept provoking them and quarrelling with them, and doing deeds that diminished him. The case that reached me was of a person aged 60. Can one imagine a man aged 60 committing behaviour resembling that of a child or a boy, and diminishing himself while he is 60?

The defect is in his religion, since he accepted for himself to consume his sisters' inheritance, and this is a defect understood by the reader. But the post speaks about the second defect. The meaning of manhood in the mind! Manhood in its correct meaning means confronting situations, not getting around them. It means clarity, whether justly or unjustly. The sixty-year-old and his brothers should not have provoked their sisters like children! Either unjustly demanding the inheritance clearly, or justly leaving it as Allah the Exalted commanded.

From idolised, misleading social concepts, many people learn that manhood means not being clear, in order to avoid situations that are not shameful, as society taught these individuals. So which is more shameful in the custom of reason and logic?

That the sixty-year-old and his brothers go to their sisters and tell them frankly and clearly of their wish to anger Allah the Exalted and disobey Him, or that they do the deeds of little boys?

In both directions there is disgrace. The first is the disgrace of the Hereafter, and the second is the disgrace of this world and sins borne through quarrelling and abuse, so the disgrace of this world and the Hereafter is achieved.

As for the wise, sensible person, he does not entangle himself in the disgrace of this world nor in the disgrace of the Hereafter!""")

